    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-275008",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\sqrt{8}=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ for $-{360}^{∘}\\le x<{0}^{∘},$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\sqrt{8} & =5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{8} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={45}^{∘}$ is not in the required domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x\\le {0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x-{360}^{∘}={45}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{315}^{∘}\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={45}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062861/assets/504427bf4d324547e1a8b142a2fce58818b32491269ce676533672a0762903b1.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={135}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x={135}^{∘}$ is not in the required domain $-{360}^{∘}\\le x\\le {0}^{∘}.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={135}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{225}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={-315}^{∘},\\,x={-225}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
